WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.520
Dance aerobics!

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:04.900
Hop, skip, and jump your way into shape!

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:28.200
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:29.980
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:31.280
And I'm Joe.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.020
Guys, why are we getting Power Pad games now?

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:37.920
I was just thinking that.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.000
We're not getting Rob.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.200
We're not getting Zapper games even.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.080
You know, it seems like they were on to something with the Zapper games.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:47.160
They're not really on to anything here with the Power Pad games.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:53.080
What's the deal? I am amazed that this peripheral is still getting support.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:01:01.800
I mean, I don't think this is a Nintendo game, so there are some third parties that still think that they can squeeze some juice out of this thing.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:03.600
Is this a Nintendo game?

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:07.120
I'm not even sure. I honestly don't remember.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:14.840
Nothing about this game will, like, you know, the first thing I think about when I think about Dance aerobics is not like, who got the copyright on the bottom of the title screen?

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.240
You know, I just didn't look.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.520
I think it was made by...

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:19.740
Oh, it is?

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.580
Okay. Nintendo, Bandai.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:23.740
Yeah. Okay.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.060
Nintendo, North America, Bandai, Japan.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.500
I don't know. Okay, that makes sense to me.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.320
Let me clear up something here.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:33.140
To me, that means Bandai made this game.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:38.080
Oh, I know. And then Nintendo was like, we'll publish this stuff for you in America.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:49.260
Because if you remember, Bandai made the Power Plus Fitness Pal, or whatever it was called, in Japan, and even released that here in the States first.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:54.240
And then, only afterwards, was Nintendo like, hey, I think they're onto something here.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:02:00.220
We should just buy the Family Fun Fitness Pad from them, and recall the Power Pad.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.660
And they should have just recalled the whole product.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:04.760
But instead, they released it like that.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:07.240
So I think that's what happened here with Dance aerobics.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:14.160
Looks like Nintendo published it, Human Entertainment, like, converted it, and Bandai originally made it.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:24.120
Yeah, so that was the, I completely missed the developer being Human Entertainment, which sounds kind of half-joking for some reason.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:28.640
I think it's like, they're obviously aliens pretending to be humans.

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:37.580
But you'll remember them from Super Team Games, and World Class Track Meet, and Athletic World.

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:46.000
Who could forget? Yeah, that's their bread and butter right there, are these, like, you know, you're going to camp, you're working out style games.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:47.920
Yeah, I love it.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:49.500
You're a human, and you're having fun.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.300
Yeah. Camp slash workout games, are there?

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:57.400
So, Dance aerobics, it's kind of like Twister, right?

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:04.400
They should have just made Twister, the game where you spin the spin board, and then it tells you, like, left foot yellow.

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:06.500
That's what this game should have been.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:11.960
I don't really know if that's correct, or if that's like a good shake.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:13.060
Oh, you're going to fight me on that that early.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:17.020
I think that that's something that you could do with the power pad.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:20.280
Yeah. And maybe they do it a little bit in this, but.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.460
It's not really, like, an aerobics thing, you know?

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:27.060
This game, I know, but this game is really just telling you, put your foot here, then put your foot there.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.620
And I think that's what Twister also tells you to do.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:39.060
But it's not, but Twister isn't just like, okay, now left foot up, left foot down, and the same button, and up and down again.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:44.320
I don't know, they're trying to make this like a Wii Fit sort of thing right now.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.680
Yeah, there's no rhythm involved in Twister.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:52.740
Yeah, yeah, there's no rhythm Twister, there's no Twister DX third cut.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:04:03.020
But I think. I'm sure, though, the odds are pretty good that, you know, you could just get your foot, left foot yellow, left foot red, left foot yellow, left foot red.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:04.200
I'm sure that could happen.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.680
Oh, it happens. In somebody's game of Twister, and that would be no different.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:10.520
Yes, yes, Mike.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:16.860
The randomness of Twister can somehow, like, turn into an aerobics routine.

00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:27.940
That's true. But can we talk about, before we even talk about this game, because I see that I've upset some people with my Twister analogy, can we just talk about the video game market in 1989 in general?

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:34.180
And, you know, you were saying that this is a lot like a Wii game, and I agree with that assessment, you know?

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:41.020
This seems like a game that is coming out way too early after a market that has clearly been established.

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:50.880
Like, if you wanted to release this game in 1985 when you're, like, trying to figure out who are video games for after the crash and stuff like that, totally get that, you know?

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:52.000
It's for everybody, right?

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:54.480
Look at this. We even have a computer buddy named Rob.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:58.500
I understand releasing, like, a family fun fitness game.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:15.500
But in 1989, where we're just consistently getting, like, games made for, like, teenage boys over and over and over again, all of a sudden for dance aerobics to come out, it feels like it feels so out of character for the system now at this point.

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:20.000
Whereas if you just glance on the Wikipedia page, you'd be like, oh, okay, they tried that and it missed.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:24.940
But now it actually, because we're playing chronologically, it feels out of place.

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:30.440
I was just going to say, because it does feel like we do get, even now we do get a sprinkling of game.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.500
I mean, Sesame Street 123 or ABC or whatever that was.

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:40.680
Classic. Felt like, yeah, it felt like also it was like, hey, they figured out a way to use this video game system, like, as a tool to teach your children.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:44.760
And, like, you know, it's not really a game in the way that we're used to.

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:46.400
And, like, this is, I don't know.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:54.140
There's still, there's still, I feel like there's still some creators out there that are like, oh, there are interesting things that you can do specifically with the power pad.

00:05:54.140 --> 00:06:02.360
Like, somebody is really believes in the power pad and knows that one day dance pads will take off and, like, is not willing to quit on it.

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:09.100
But, like, I do feel like, yeah, once in a while there's always, like, a weird game every, like, 15 games or something.

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:15.720
That completely breaks the rules of what a game is so far, for better or worse.

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:17.360
No, I agree with that.

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:32.280
But in terms of this specific game and within the ecosystem of dance pad games, I think this is the, like, the best application so far.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:39.140
I think that the ones that were, like, you're, like, they give you the story that you're at camp and this is you running.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:46.120
And you just have to stomp on these two buttons over and over and to jump, you have to jump.

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:49.080
But, like, this is more one-to-one.

00:06:49.420 --> 00:07:00.420
Like, you could argue that the game can't really enforce that you do anything with your arms like our little friend that teaches us everything.

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:11.420
But everything else is, like, it's more of a tool than, was it team games or whatever it was?

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:12.620
Yeah, super team games.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:20.920
Yeah. Okay, well, for one, you just, you clearly stole my notes for this episode and you hit every single bullet point that I was about to go through for the remainder of the episode.

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:22.380
So, folks, it's all done now.

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:23.880
Yeah, that's the game. You might as well just close it out.

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:26.400
But, no, seriously, this one works.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:31.180
I actually was proud of this game while I was playing it.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:40.420
Like, I wouldn't say I had a great time, but I was proud of, like, wow, they finally figured out a useful demonstration of how a power pad could work.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:47.640
At varying skill levels, too, because there is, like, warm-ups and then there's, like, a full 20-minute programmed routine.

00:07:47.640 --> 00:08:04.560
So, there's no arguing that what they've set out to do here, simple as it may be or inconsequential as it may be, you know, if the goal is just to simulate exercising while also making it fun with video graphics, I think they've done that here.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:09.380
Yeah, this is the mirror, pretty much.

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:13.560
You know that stupid mirror with video.

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:15.920
Oh, yeah, yeah. Took me a second.

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:18.300
I was like, I think this is more advanced than a mirror, but no.

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:23.220
Yeah, no, the one that actually, like, shows you on it with your stats and everything, right.

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:26.400
And you know what? You can't really cheat in this game.

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:28.780
Like, at first I was like, the whole game is cheating.

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:30.860
You don't have to play this game.

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:35.360
You could buy the cartridge, put it in, never have the power pad, right?

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:36.760
You don't have to even own the power pad.

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:47.500
And because there's no game over and there's no, like, technically it does count the amount of mistakes you've made, but it won't, like, bump you out of the session if you make too many mistakes.

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:52.420
Technically, you could just copy the instructions just on your carpet.

00:08:52.420 --> 00:08:56.260
Like, you never need to use any kind of power pad or anything.

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:57.880
You can just mimic the moves.

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:05.400
So you really can't cheat because the only way to cheat in this game would be to just let it run and not do anything.

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:06.520
Like, just sit on the couch.

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:09.900
Well, it'll let you continue the, like, the level.

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:15.440
If you don't put any inputs in, it's not going to send you to the next level.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:17.060
Yeah, you won't get the stamp.

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:18.920
Yeah, it does tell you your mistakes.

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:24.440
Also, I was thinking, you know, if you cheat at this game, I mean, you've just played yourself.

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:25.980
Like, just don't play this game.

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:30.960
There's no reason to play this game if you don't want to actually do the, you know, do the thing that it's made for.

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:36.660
You know what I mean? Like, it's not like, oh, I just want to get to that next level, you know, of this dungeon in whatever game I'm playing.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:38.800
It's just like, you bought this game to do aerobics.

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:40.060
So, like, just do aerobics.

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:43.100
If you're going to cheat, then, like, I just don't think you want to play this game.

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:53.460
But you know somebody, like, real smug, like, bought the game, did, like, the first five-minute workout, but sat on the couch and just ate chips for all the dance aerobic thing was going on.

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:57.880
All the while, they think they're passing the level because it's not saying game over or anything.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:02.000
And then, to Sean's point, you don't get the stamp pass.

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:06.040
So, you know, like your hole punch card, you don't have that punch.

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:07.480
So, now you have to do that again.

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:11.880
So, nobody really wins unless you burn a sweat in this game.

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:26.560
Right. That's right. Also, I wanted to mention, Sean, or one of you guys mentioned, I can't tell you guys apart, but one of you guys mentioned that you can just do whatever you want with your hands.

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:28.180
Like, you don't actually have to do the things with your hands.

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:33.720
That's still something that, like, is done today, like, with games like this.

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:35.580
Or not today, but much more recently.

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:43.000
I remember my sister had, I think it was Just Dance or one of those dance games on the Wii where you just used the Wiimote.

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:45.300
So, that was it.

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:47.600
It just tracked the motion of your right arm.

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:50.200
But it was, like, a whole dance routine you had to do.

00:10:50.580 --> 00:11:04.800
So, like, if there's still, like, if people are still buying into that today and saying, like, yeah, this is, like, this is working, then, like, I think that, honestly, the dance pad feels like it's even more, like, immersive than doing it on the Wii.

00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:13.280
And that also isn't the only, I'm sorry, because it's not entirely correct that you can do whatever you want with your hands.

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:22.900
Because there are certain routines that require you to bend down and touch a button on the pad while still having your feet on the ground.

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:27.580
So, I guess most of the time you don't really have to do anything with your hands.

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:29.880
But other than that, I do agree with you, yeah.

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:35.760
And we can fantasize, like, a bunch of different ways on how to cheat at this game, right?

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:40.720
But ultimately, like, you're the person who paid for the game, right?

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:50.260
And now you're putting it in there, like, there's nothing worse than, like, either brute forcing or half-assing, like, a video game that you own.

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:56.660
You know, in a certain way, like, that actually, you're the one robbing yourself of the experience here.

00:11:56.660 --> 00:12:00.820
So, I know what Joe's talking about because there are plenty of Wii game examples.

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:10.520
I think even, like, real video games on Wii, like, technically Skyward Sword, you know, that, like, requires you to slash in certain directions or certain enemies.

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:20.100
And there are people who will go, like, all in on those slashes and do, like, a very, like, swordsman-like diagonal slash to make sure that it registers.

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:26.240
And then there are other people who will just, like, flick their wrist diagonally, you know, and they'll be like, you know, and just keep doing it.

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:28.700
It's like, who's really getting the reward there?

00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:36.280
I think the guy who, like, goes all in on, like, owning that his Wii-mote is the sword is the guy who's actually getting something out of his game.

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:46.120
Right. But in that, like, that other guy who's just flicking his wrist, if that's the way he wants to play it, like, I get that for Skyward Sword, for something like that.

00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:47.080
He wants to go through the story.

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:49.040
He wants to just kind of use it almost like a button.

00:12:49.580 --> 00:12:55.120
I don't get somebody cheating at dance aerobics because you're not getting anything else out of it other than aerobics.

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:58.960
Like, at least Skyward Sword, you're getting the story, you're getting other elements of this gameplay, you're getting the puzzle.

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:03.660
This, you get nothing out of it if you do anything other than, like, try and do the aerobics.

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:12.400
The funny thing about this game is I'm sure that all three of us didn't play it as intended because we could never get that pad to work.

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:15.140
That's true. We all sort of cheated, actually.

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:16.300
Yeah, we all cheated.

00:13:16.300 --> 00:13:20.400
And I just played this game on an array on my keyboard.

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:26.700
So I guess I wasn't getting too many aerobics, but definitely get my fingers a workout.

00:13:27.620 --> 00:13:28.800
I did the aerobics.

00:13:28.900 --> 00:13:29.720
I played on the carpet.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:33.220
You know, like I said, you don't have to own a power pad to do this.

00:13:33.940 --> 00:13:43.880
I don't know what's more funny, the fact that we play with our fingers to actually play the game or you play on the carpet, not actually interacting with the game.

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:46.820
It's no different than playing stack-up.

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:56.560
Exactly. You know, but I think, like, that's what this whole thing comes down to, too, is, like, you can cheat at Dance Aerobics.

00:13:56.700 --> 00:13:58.640
You can cheat at Wii Fit, right?

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:11.260
Like, the scale doesn't lie, but, like, you can play the minigames and just, you know, do the running thing by, like, slapping your hands, you know, in, like, a kneeling position to run really fast instead of actually moving your feet.

00:14:11.260 --> 00:14:18.180
Like, there are all sorts of ways to get creative about this, but it doesn't really matter because the game's objective is just to get you moving.

00:14:18.460 --> 00:14:23.880
Like, I think there's a disclaimer in the manual that's like, please don't play this game for, like, more than an hour a day.

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:27.460
Like, you know, like, you're going to work up a sweat.

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:29.760
That's, like, what the game is selling you here.

00:14:29.820 --> 00:14:30.860
It's selling you a workout.

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:32.620
It's not a video game.

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:37.280
We shouldn't be talking about all the different ways to cheat because it doesn't even matter.

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:40.560
The objective is something that the game doesn't track here.

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:46.720
The game is trying to keep you in shape, and it has no way of knowing if you're getting better shape or not.

00:14:47.300 --> 00:14:49.840
It's true. You're on the honor system.

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:52.980
Yeah, they should have built in a scale to the power pad.

00:14:54.200 --> 00:14:56.080
The menu bothered me a little bit.

00:14:56.240 --> 00:14:57.940
It just says, which would you like?

00:14:58.120 --> 00:15:02.460
I don't think that that's appropriate language for the menu, you know.

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:05.120
It should just present the options.

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:06.720
Which would you like?

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:08.760
And then just four options?

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:15.280
I don't know. Something about the wording of that just feels like a bad translation or just, like, a straightforward translation.

00:15:15.980 --> 00:15:16.860
Which would you like?

00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:18.440
I've never heard anybody say that to me before.

00:15:19.180 --> 00:15:25.600
You know, if somebody offered me a variety of options on something.

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:28.420
Okay, no. If somebody offered you food, maybe.

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:30.920
I'm talking about, like, someone offers you four game choices.

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:32.260
They're like, which would you like?

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:34.660
Well, what if somebody was like, hey, do you want a free board game?

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:36.260
I've got four here. Which would you like?

00:15:36.700 --> 00:15:37.760
But I bought the game.

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:39.720
I'd like all of the options, right?

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:41.480
I'm going to play for all of them.

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:44.060
So do you want to say, like, which would you like to play first?

00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:45.220
Do you want it to be longer?

00:15:46.300 --> 00:15:48.000
How about just, like, select your mode?

00:15:48.700 --> 00:15:50.340
So you want them to be more dominant.

00:15:51.080 --> 00:15:52.060
Yeah. Yeah, exactly.

00:15:52.580 --> 00:15:58.260
I just wanted that every button just takes you down the list and then it loops back around no matter what you want.

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:02.980
No, no, no, no. I just wanted to act like a fitness trainer that is bullying me.

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:06.060
You know, it's like, will you just pick a mode already?

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:09.800
Immediately. We've got work to do.

00:16:10.620 --> 00:16:15.980
Well, some of the other things that are said, like, I think, I'm just watching a video right now.

00:16:16.140 --> 00:16:20.320
And the trainer says in between, try again, relax, and improve.

00:16:21.380 --> 00:16:23.440
It's very, I don't know, it's kind of like.

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:25.300
It's very supportive.

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:29.000
I didn't know. Well, it also says relax and improve, though.

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:32.100
I don't know. It's just funny to me.

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:35.520
It implies, like, she didn't say, hey, you didn't do great there.

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:36.400
She just said improve.

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:48.900
